Finding: {"file":"src/portable/microchip/samx7x/dcd_samx7x.c","line":539,"snippet":"USB_REG->DEVEPTICR[epnum] = DEVEPTICR_TXINIC;
      if (USB_REG->DEVEPTISR[epnum] & DEVEPTISR_NBUSYBK) {
        xfer->zlp_pending         = true;
        USB_REG->DEVEPTIER[epnum] = DEVEPTIER_TXINES;
      } else {
        dcd_transmit_packet(xfer, epnum);
      }","why":"This is a check-then-act race against the hardware. The code clears TXINI, then reads NBUSYBK in a separate access. If the last busy bank (for example one validated by a finished DMA IN transfer, which runs with TXINE masked) is freed between the two accesses, hardware sets TXINI again and NBUSYBK reads 0. The else path then clears FIFOCON to validate the ZLP and enables TXINE while TXINI is still set from the earlier bank. The PEP interrupt fires at once, and dcd_ep_handler sees zlp_pending==false and queued_len==total_len==0, so it reports the IN transfer complete while the ZLP is still in the bank. dcd_edpt_xfer normally runs in task context with interrupts enabled, so any other endpoint's ISR that preempts between the TXINIC write and the NBUSYBK read makes the window much wider. On a single-bank endpoint the stack can then queue the next IN packet. dcd_transmit_packet memcpys into the still-busy bank and clears FIFOCON, which is a no-op on a busy bank (the driver's own comment says so), so the packet is lost or corrupted and a completion is reported for data never sent. This diff reverts dc8d107c22, whose commit message describes exactly this race ('the last busy bank could free between the TXINI acknowledge and the NBUSYBK read, and the fresh TXINI then reported the ZLP complete while it still sat in the bank'). Datasheet DS60001527J section 38.6.2.12, p761 (book 6165, lookup 6e83c09aac8f) says TXINI is set by hardware when the current bank becomes free and must be cleared before FIFOCON is cleared, which confirms TXINI can be stale at TXINES time. The SAM E70 errata DS80000767 (book 6073, lookup f2a806869a14) has no USBHS item that covers this.","severity":"medium","confidence":"medium"}
